#!/usr/bin/python3
# Written by Hydra: the Hydra installer ("Install Hydra" on the live system's desktop).
#   hydra-install [--screenshots DIR]
# A few clear steps, like Windows Setup: language, keyboard, time zone, the disk (the whole disk
# is erased; the stick Hydra runs from is never offered), your account, a summary, then the
# installation with its progress in plain words, and "Restart now".
# The disk is written by /usr/libexec/hydra-install-helper, run as root through polkit (pkexec;
# the live system's user "live" needs no password). This window never sees root rights; the
# password is turned into a crypt(3) hash (yescrypt, as /etc/shadow keeps it) here, and only
# the hash leaves this program. The settings are applied at the first start of the new computer
# (hydra-firstboot).
# --screenshots DIR: fills in sample answers and saves a picture of every page to DIR (for
# Hydra's tests; nothing is installed).
# For tests on disk image files: HYDRA_INSTALL_TEST=1 runs the helper directly (as the calling
# user, who must be root) instead of through pkexec, so HYDRA_INSTALL_TEST_DISKS and
# HYDRA_INSTALL_SOURCE reach it; HYDRA_INSTALL_HELPER names another helper.
